{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born in 1130 or 1131 to Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His father Raymond II was the Count of Tripoli and his mother Hodierna was the daughter of King Baldwin II of Jerusalem. Raymond III became the Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father. He was only 21 years old at the time.\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate. In 1153 he allied with the Kingdom of Jerusalem to capture the Fatimid city of Ascalon. However the alliance was short-lived as Raymond III and the Kingdom of Jerusalem clashed over control of the city of Tortosa.\n\n*The Battle of Inab*\nIn 1149 Raymond III was captured by the Zengid dynasty during the Battle of Inab. He was held captive for several years before being released in 1153. During his captivity the regent of the County of Tripoli was his mother Hodierna.\n\n*The Regency of Raymond III*\nRaymond III's mother Hodierna played an important role in his early reign. She acted as regent of the County of Tripoli during his captivity and continued to advise him after his release. However Raymond III eventually took full control of the county and his mother's influence waned.\n\nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and he expanded the County of Tripoli through several military campaigns. He also strengthened the county's defenses and improved its economy. However his reign was also marked by conflicts with neighboring states and the constant threat of Muslim invasion.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. He was remembered as a wise and just ruler who strengthened the County of Tripoli and expanded its territories.", "logprobs